---
source: crates/surfaces/cli/src/main.rs
expression: command.clone().render_long_help().to_string()
---
The files and directories under a directory, honouring .gitignore

Usage: specforge files list [OPTIONS] [DIR]

Arguments:
  [DIR]
          The directory, from the current directory
          
          [default: .]

Options:
      --glob <GLOB>
          Only entries that match one of these globs: one with a `/` is matched against the path below the directory, one without against the entry's name

      --kind <KIND>
          Only files, or only directories
          
          [possible values: file, dir]

      --depth <N>
          How deep below the directory to list (1: its own entries)

      --no-ignore
          List what .gitignore and .ignore leave out too

      --hidden
          List names that start with `.`

      --max <N>
          The most entries listed
          
          [default: 10000]

      --path <PATH>
          A path inside the project (the nearest directory at or above it holding specforge.json; none: the directory itself, with the default configuration)
          
          [default: .]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: json (the default) or human
          
          [default: json]
          [possible values: human, json]

      --pick <PATH>
          Print only the values at this path of the verb's JSON answer, one per line, e.g. '.entries[].path' on files list or '.branch' on git head. The path is steps: `.key`, `[n]` (digits, no leading zero), `[]` (every item) and `[<key>=<value>]` (the items whose field <key> is <value>, e.g. `[kind=plan]`: <key> may be dotted, `[fields.result=issue]`, and a number, boolean or null is compared as JSON, `[fields.backlog=true]`, `[fields.wave=2]`; an object or array is never selected); a `.` before `[` may be left out, and `.` alone is the whole answer. One line per value reached: a string bare (a newline in it is printed as is), an integer as is and any other number as an f64 (`1.0`, `-0.0`, `1e3` as `1000.0`), a boolean, object or array as compact JSON; null or a missing value prints nothing, and is not an error. It takes no --format human: errors print as they do under json, and the exit code is the verb's.

      --effects <EFFECTS>
          The effects mode: online (the default; auto is online), cached or replay. http sends the request, answers a GET from a fresh record of the effects journal (cached), or answers from the journal alone (replay); files and git read and write this machine's disk in every mode, and the journal keeps none of it

          Possible values:
          - online: Performed when granted; the answer is journaled
          - auto:   A fresh record; else performed when approved; else not performed
          - cached: A fresh record; else not performed
          - replay: Any record, expired or not; else a miss

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
